x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{6}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{6}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{6}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{6}^{-2u}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{6}^{-2u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{6}^{2x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056962/assets/8f732515dd376db40889706419781e02cbedfcd4da8513a09e4f2d77ff88ece2.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186317074 17592186334588 17592186334591] "immutable_fields" [[17592186317073 :answer-field/correct] [17592186317073 :answ